Setting (exact commutative algebra over a field k, char 0, alg. closed where needed).

Fix a dimension vector d = (d_0, ..., d_N) of positive integers and a target rank r with
r <= min_i d_i. Consider the affine space Rep_d of tuples A = (A_1, ..., A_N) of matrices,
A_i of size d_i x d_{i-1}, and the product P(A) = A_N * A_{N-1} * ... * A_1 (a d_N x d_0 matrix).

Sigma^r := { A in Rep_d : rank P(A) <= r }, the "rank-<=r product locus". Its ideal
sigmaIdeal in the polynomial ring k[Rep_d] is generated by all (r+1)x(r+1) minors of the
generic product matrix P (entries = polynomials in the matrix entries).

Define detDelta(A) := determinant of the TOP-LEFT r x r submatrix of P(A) (rows 1..r, cols 1..r).
This is a single fixed polynomial in k[Rep_d] (the "canonical pivot minor").

KNOWN STRUCTURE (established facts I am working from, not to be re-derived):
- The irreducible components of Sigma^r are orbit closures Ō_M for the GL-product group acting
  by base change, indexed by "Kostant partitions" m of d at corner r. Each component is the
  "orbit-rank-locus" of a realizer M_m (a representative whose product has rank exactly r).
- The TOP-DIMENSIONAL components (= minimal codimension among the components) are indexed by the
  *minimising* Kostant partitions; their count is a combinatorial number theta = C(m, |delta|).
- For a realizer M whose product P(M) has rank exactly r, two matrices of equal rank are
  GL x GL-equivalent (an end-factor base change carries P(M) to the normal form diag(I_r, 0)).
  The base-changed tuple stays in the same orbit-rank-locus (rank-pattern is base-change invariant).

THE QUESTION (one sharp truth-value, in one direction):
Does detDelta avoid every top-dimensional component? I.e. for every top-dimensional minimal prime
p of sigmaIdeal, is detDelta NOT in p (detDelta does not vanish identically on that component)?

Sub-questions I specifically want an independent read on:
1. Is the claim TRUE in general, and what is the cleanest structural reason (or the cleanest
   potential counterexample mechanism if FALSE)?
2. CRUCIAL: does the SINGLE canonical top-left r x r minor detDelta suffice for ALL top
   components simultaneously (one global localization), or could there be a top component on which
   the top-left r x r minor vanishes identically while some OTHER r x r minor does not (forcing a
   per-component / partition-dependent pivot)? Distinguish "detDelta is nonzero at the realizer
   POINT itself" from "detDelta is nonzero somewhere on the component" — only the latter is needed.
3. Is there any d, r where a top-dimensional component sits entirely at product-rank < r (so the
   normal form diag(I_r,0) is NOT in its closure), which would break the avoidance? Relate to the
   non-equidimensional cases (e.g. (2,2,3) at r=1 has a lower-dim component).

FACTS FROM MY OWN EXACT COMPUTATION (Singular minAssGTZ over Q), for the SOURCE Sigma^r side:
- (2,2,2) r=1: 2 top comps (det A_i=0), codim 1, detDelta = P[1,1] not in either prime. holds.
- (2,2,2,2) r=1: 3 top comps, codim 1, detDelta not in any. holds.
- (3,3,3) r=2: 2 top comps, codim 1, detDelta (top-left 2x2 minor) not in any. holds.
- (3,3,3) r=1: 3 minimal primes, 1 top (codim 3), detDelta not in it. holds.
- (3,3,3,3) r=2: 3 top comps, detDelta not in any. holds.
- (2,2,3) r=1: 2 minimal primes (dims 8,9), 1 top (codim 1, det A_1=0), detDelta not in it. holds.
- (2,2,2,2,2) r=0: detDelta = det(empty)=1, a unit, in no prime (vacuous).
